<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Focal epilepsy with structural cause, seizure-free after lesionectomy (G40.209)</content>. Seizure-free for six and a half months on levetiracetam, four months after complete lesionectomy. In California the decision to drive rests with the DMV, which reviews her physician&apos;s report of seizure control; she meets the usual seizure-free period for its review. Levetiracetam continues for at least two years after surgery; a taper would then be a shared decision with the epilepsy team, with an EEG first. [S]<list><item>Medical report to the DMV supporting a review of her driving, at her request.</item><item>Continue levetiracetam 500 mg twice daily and folic acid.</item><item>Keep the detection watch and the water safety rules for now.</item><item>Plan any pregnancy with the epilepsy team; levetiracetam can continue in pregnancy.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure diary and adherence</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No seizure for six and a half months; no watch alerts; every dose taken</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Mood and behavior on levetiracetam (PHQ-9)</td><td>Before starting and at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 2</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving restriction and the California physician report</td><td>Until cleared by the DMV</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Medical report sent today, at her request, for the DMV&apos;s review</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Repeat MRI</td><td>After complete resection, only for new symptoms</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Complete resection and no symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Repeat EEG</td><td>Before any taper of antiseizure medication</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No taper is planned for at least two years</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Nocturnal seizure detection</td><td>While on treatment after recent seizures</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Continues by her choice</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>SUDEP risk review</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No tonic-clonic seizure for six and a half months, the main protection; she knows the plan</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Folic acid, contraception and pregnancy planning</td><td>Each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No pregnancy planned this year; plan with the epilepsy team</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>EEG and MRI with susceptibility-weighted imaging within one week, and an epilepsy consultation within two weeks. Outcome by 2026-05-05: EEG in two days, MRI in six, consultation at two weeks [S]</item><item>No further seizures on an antiseizure medication she tolerates. Outcome by 2026-10-20: No seizure in six months; levetiracetam tolerated after early irritability settled [S]</item><item>Neurosurgical evaluation of the cavernous malformation and a shared decision about lesionectomy within two months. Outcome by 2026-07-21: Seen by neurosurgery at three weeks; complete lesionectomy at eight weeks [S]</item><item>Safety plan in place: no driving until cleared, water and height safety, SUDEP discussion, and a nocturnal seizure detection plan. Outcome by 2026-05-27: Driving stopped and reported as California requires; SUDEP discussed (one visit late, recorded as a deviation); detection watch in use [S]</item><item>Graded return to full sprint training within four months of surgery. Outcome by 2026-10-20: Full training at four months after surgery [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Krumholz A, et al. Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults. Neurology 2015;84(16):1705-1713 (PMID 25901057) (2015); Akers A, et al. Synopsis of Guidelines for the Clinical Management of Cerebral Cavernous Malformations: Consensus Recommendations Based on Systematic Literature Review by the Angioma Alliance Scientific Advisory Board Clinical Experts Panel. Neurosurgery 2017;80(5):665-680 (PMID 28387823) (2017); Harden C, et al. Practice guideline summary: Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy incidence rates and risk factors. Neurology 2017;88(17):1674-1680 (PMID 28438841) (2017); Pack AM, et al. Teratogenesis, Perinatal, and Neurodevelopmental Outcomes After In Utero Exposure to Antiseizure Medication: Practice Guideline From the AAN, AES, and SMFM. Neurology 2024;102(11):e209279 (PMID 38748979) (2024); Beniczky S, et al. Automated seizure detection using wearable devices: A clinical practice guideline of the International League Against Epilepsy and the International Federation of Clinical Neurophysiology. Epilepsia 2021;62(3):632-646 (PMID 33666944) (2021); Governor&apos;s message returning Senate Bill 357 without signature (physician reporting of disorders characterized by lapses of consciousness remains mandatory under Health and Safety Code section 103900) (2024); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Epilepsy review in six months; primary care preventive visit yearly. Call at once for any seizure; no driving until the DMV clears her. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
